# Trim the docker build context so `docker build` ships only what the
# image actually needs to compile from. Per PRD #486 slice 5 / issue
# #491, this file exists to:
#   - keep the build fast (small context = small upload to the daemon)
#   - keep the build deterministic (rebuild artifacts inside the image
#     instead of importing host-built ones)
#   - keep secrets and local dev state out of the build context entirely
#
# Anything not listed here is allowed in. The Dockerfile copies what
# it needs explicitly; everything else is just available if a stage
# wants it.

# --- build artifacts (rebuilt inside the image) ---------------------
target/
target_*/
**/target/
.cargo-target/
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
**/.vite/
lucida-web/dist/
lucida-core/pkg

# --- local dev SQLite (must NOT enter the image) --------------------
# These are the WAL/SHM/main DB files lucida creates on first run;
# leaking one into the image would ship some developer's session and
# saved-view rows to every adopter.
lucida.db
lucida.db-shm
lucida.db-wal

# --- env files / secrets --------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
*.oauth.json
*client_secret*.json

# --- VCS + dev-host metadata ----------------------------------------
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github/
.claude/

# --- documentation (not in the image) -------------------------------
wiki/
# Top-level docs only (README.md / CHUNK_PIPELINE.md / CLAUDE.md /
# AGENTS.md). Leading slash anchors at the build-context root so
# any future per-crate README.md keeps shipping into the build.
/*.md

# --- adopter-side artifacts (applied by the adopter, not bundled) ---
extras/

# --- workspace members not in the image -----------------------------
# lucida-py is excluded from the rust workspace
# (`exclude = ["lucida-py"]` in the root Cargo.toml) and is not part
# of the lucida-server image.
lucida-py/

# --- editor / OS noise ----------------------------------------------
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store
*.log
*.swp
*.swo

# --- the Dockerfile itself doesn't need to be inside the context ----
# (docker still reads it from disk; this just keeps it out of any
# COPY . that a stage might do.)
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
